<section image-layout="blank" id="B-uYnOS4oX" vertical-align="center" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.38"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Mg_38a9d0yi7UoPDDzimc.png" saved-media-id="yp22nbuc3imcvwu" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="celestial-copperplate" alt="Digitally illustrated copperplate celestial engraving of a brass telescope aimed across a vast deep-space field, precise ivory coordinate arcs widening across an abyssal-blue sky, cyan spectral traces and one restrained coral signal, a tiny generic ship silhouette low at the far right for scale, generous dark negative space on the left, no central explosion, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="LPXLeEPBIr"><p role="eyebrow">No shore, no center</p><h1 font-size="52pt" style="padding-right: 34%;">Captain, reverse the telescope</h1><p font-size="lg" style="padding-right: 42%;"><b>Across distant galaxies, one clue repeats:</b> the universe is not debris racing from a blast point. Space itself has been stretching for about 13.8 billion years.</p><spacer size="24pt"></spacer><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="xs"><p font-size="sm">Our course follows three bearings · Expansion · Cooling · Evidence</p></grid-item></grid></section>